\par\par \b \uc2  Scotland's Baillie Gifford backs Japan for 1996. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Bill Magee \par \uc2 364 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 1995\par 18:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  EDINBURGH, Nov 22 (Reuter) - Scottish fund manager Baillie Gifford is backing investment in Japan for 1996, saying the economy is finally about to emerge from its most prolonged downturn since World War Two. \par \par \uc2  Malcolm Murray, chairman of the 74.6 million stg Baillie Gifford Japan Trust, said in a trading update that the funds' main holdings were spread over the whole Japanese equity market. \par \par \uc2  "There are grounds for believing conditions will start to improve," Murray said. "The recent decline in the yen, reduction in interest rates to their lowest level ever, a boost from Kobe rebuilding and recent annoucements of huge fiscal stimulus packages all suggest an economic pick-up over coming months." \par \par \uc2  The fund is invested in sectors ranging from electronic parts and pharmaceuticals to telecoms, media, property and shipbuilding. \par \par \uc2  Trust manager Sarah Whitley said there had been a decisive change in Japanese policy during the last three months. \par \par \uc2  "This has improved prospects for the economy and stockmarket and averted the possibility of a deflationary downturn," she said. \par \par \uc2  It also had the overt support of other members of the G7 group of most industrialised nations, as shown by concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's manufacturing companies had in general dealt with the legacy of the bubble much sooner than financial firms, reflected in profits. "We continue to look for further reform and deregulation," she said. \par \par \uc2  Further moves would go a long way towards boosting long-term growth, which at present was negligible. "A serious effort at dealing with several problem areas has begun, which makes us more positive about the outlook for equities." \par \par \uc2  New holdings by the Baillie Gifford Trust, now in its 15th year, include Bandai toy manufacturers, Nippon Shokubai chemicals and Sankyo (Pachinko) machines. \par \par \uc2  Portfolio distribution by sector is electricals 20.3 percent, manufacturing and machinery 19.3 percent, retail 9.7 percent, commerce and services 12.6 percent, pharmaceuticals and food 6.4 percent, real estate, housing and construction 4.6 percent, financial and utilities, 14.9 percent, chemicals and other materials 6.3 percent. \par \par \uc2  -- London newsroom +44 171 510 6414 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103drbm01ize\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
