\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 /Foreign Reserves -4: Indication Of BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 185 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 1995\par 11:15\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  'Broadly speaking, the increase in foreign reserves is a very good indicator of the amount of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,' said C.H. Kwan, senior economist at Nomura Research Institute. \par \par \uc2  The accumulation of reserves 'by definition' shows the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, buying dollars and selling yen, in an effort to support the dollar, he said. He said the rise also includes some interest income on foreign securities and other factors, 'but the difference is negligible.' \par \par \uc2  The BOJ, Kwan said, 'is perhaps the only major \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world willing to accumulate excess dollars.' \par \par \uc2  The BOJ was confirmed buying dollars in the Tokyo market several times in October. However, the dollar rarely came under major selling pressure in a mostly quiet month for dollar-yen trading. \par \par \uc2  A weak dollar is a bane to Japan's export-dependant economy because it tends to make Japanese exports less competitive in world markets and to reduce the value of overseas earnings in yen terms. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 01-11-95 \par \par \uc2  1015GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025drb108jh8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
