\par\par \b \uc2  Japan's huge trade surplus falls slightly. \b0\par\par\uc2 413 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Oct 18 (Reuter) - Japan's huge customs-cleared trade surplus narrowed to $56.19 billion in the first half of this fiscal year from a revised $59.21 billion surplus in the same period a year earlier, the finance ministry said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Despite the 5.1 percent drop, the surplus figures still helped push the dollar below 100 yen. It later rebounded back over that figure, helped by what bankers said was Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's politically sensitive trade surplus with the United States fell 13.9 percent to $23.32 billion in the six months to September from $27.08 billion the same period a year ago. Figures are not adjusted for seasonal factors. \par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said the figures showed the surplus was continuing its downward trend. \par \par \uc2  "Basically, our trade surplus is on a declining trend. We hope that the surplus will decline further with the implementation of government economic stimulus packages such as the one announced on September 20," the official said. \par \par \uc2  In September alone, Japan's trade surplus fell to $11.29 billion from a revised $11.96 billion a year earlier, marking a third straight month of year-on-year declines. In August, the surplus stood at $5.97 billion. \par \par \uc2  Economists had forecast the September surplus would fall to between $9.7 billion and $10.6 billion from a year ago, partly reflecting the recent weakness of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but were not shocked it was slightly higher than their predictions. \par \par \uc2  "I was not surprised to see the figure," said Tetsuro Sawano, an economist at Nikko Research Center. \par \par \uc2  "The decreasing trend in the surplus is still on its way, and we foresee (the figure for) fiscal 1995/96 will be a much smaller number than several years ago, so we don't worry about a further possible increase in the trade balance," said Yuichi Matsushita, manager of global strategy at Nikko Securities. \par \par \uc2  The yen has fallen sharply from a high of 79.75 yen to the dollar in April to near its level of a year ago, losing its previous effect in boosting the dollar-based value of Japanese exports, economists have said. \par \par \uc2  With the volume of imports tending to grow at a much faster pace than that of exports, the shrinking trend in the surplus will stay solid for the time being, the economists said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103drai02rwk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
