\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Of Japan/99.20 Yen -2: May Be Only Major Buyer \b0\par\par\uc2 139 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 1995\par 02:49\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (AP-DJ)--Traders say the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, and around 0043 GMT was buying dollars around 99.20 yen. \par \par \uc2  They said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been an active buyer all morning and may in fact be the only major buyer in the market. \par \par \uc2  A trader at a major Tokyo bank said the BOJ alone won't be able to stop the dollar from sliding Friday, though he said it can probably keep the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  supported above 98.00 yen in Asian time. \par \par \uc2  He said a descent toward 96.00 yen is likely later in the global day if other G-7 central banks don't join the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 21-09-95 \par \par \uc2  0049GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025dr9m07d2f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
