\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar extends rally with endorsement of U.S., Germany, Japan. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Marjorie Olster \par \uc2 780 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 15 (Reuter) - The dollar extended its gains against the Japanese yen to another 15-month peak, bolstered by fresh endorsements of the rally from the world's three largest economic powers. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers, Bundesbank President Hans Tietmeyer and Japanese Minster for International Trade and Industry Ryutaro Hashimoto all separately indicated they favoured further dollar appreciation. \par \par \uc2  Some market analysts however said the three may have been trying to allay concerns that they do not want to see the dollar rise as rapidly as it has been recently. \par \par \uc2  The officials appeared to be reaffirming their commitment to a landmark Group of Seven (G7) communique issued in April vowing to work for the orderly reversal of the dollar's decline against major world \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It was also a clear signal that they see room for further gains. \par \par \uc2  Hashimoto was quoted in the Japanese newspaper Nihon Keisai Shimbun saying he favoured the dollar's rise to 110 yen. \par \par \uc2  Summers said in a U.S. television interview he welcomed the dollar's rise and added: "We're prepared to cooperate in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets with our G7 partners to continue that reversal (of the dollar's decline)." \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Bundesbank President Hans Tietmeyer told reporters after a Franco-German summit he hopes for continued corrections of distortions in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates and for a "global calming" of those rates. \par \par \uc2  "It took people a little by surprise, to come out so strongly simultaneoulsy," said Amy Smith, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at consulting firm I.D.E.A. Inc. "It led everyone to think it was a warmup for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  The central banks did not step in however, leading some investors to take profits and bringing the dollar off highs above 104 yen and just shy of 1.50 German marks. \par \par \uc2  On Friday afternoon, the dollar was trading at 1.4890 marks, up slightly from 1.4861 late Thursday. It rose to 103.95 yen from 102.40 Thursday. It made another new 15-month high above 104 yen, above the level Wednesday that was the highest since June 1994. \par \par \uc2  Analysts viewed Summer's comments as an attempt to allay any fears that the U.S. may back off in its support for a higher dollar because such sharp upward moves could hurt the profitability of U.S. exporters and affect the stock market. \par \par \uc2  "They like the fact the dollar is gaining but they can't be wild about how fast it is doing it," said Smith. \par \par \uc2  Some detected a note of concern in Tietmeyer's comments about the mark's spike against the yen recently. That cross soared to a new 29-month high Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The bullish statements (by Tietmeyer) were a function of the fact they were a little concerned about the velocity of the yen weakening against the mark," said Peggy Reed, vice president of institutional sales at Credit Agricole. "They'd prefer to see mark/yen not move as aggressively as it has." \par \par \uc2  The mark once again proved resilient as attempts to push the dollar above 1.50 failed. That only reinforced the view that this rally is more a function of yen weakness than dollar strength. European accounts were said to have been selling dollars for marks. \par \par \uc2  There were widespread rumours the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was aggressively selling dollars for marks just below the 1.50 level to protect a large options position. \par \par \uc2  Analysts expect the dollar to remain well supported against the yen ahead of a Japanese fiscal package due next Wednesday and expected to be accompanied by aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and possibly by the U.S. Federal Reserve. \par \par \uc2  The market is expecting a roughly 10-trillion-yen package coupled with additional deregulation of overseas investment. \par \par \uc2  New U.S. economic data had a mixed effect on dollar. \par \par \uc2  The dollar firmed on a robust 1.1 percent rise in August industrial production and capacity use rose 0.6 percent to 84.3. Economists said that number reinforced the view that the Fed will not cut U.S. interest rates at its September 26 Federal Open Market Committee meeting. \par \par \uc2  Lower U.S. interest rates could diminish the attractiveness of the dollar and other U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  However a drop of 7.3 in the University of Michigan September consumer sentiment index to 88.9 was more bearish for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  In other trading, the dollar fell to 1.2080 Swiss francs from 1.2125 Thursday. The British pound firmed to $1.5503 from $1.5483 Thursday while the dollar fell to Canadian $1.3660 from $1.3707. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr9f03t5v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
