\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar tops 103 yen level but slips into retreat. \b0\par\par\uc2 587 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 14 (Reuter) - The dollar marked another milestone in its comeback against the yen on Friday, rising above the 103 yen level but later retreating to lower ground in the face of fierce profit-taking. \par \par \uc2  In a day of dramatic swings on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the dollar sprinted through the 103 yen mark on a wave of early buying but, its energy flaging, it slipped back sharply before finally settling at 102.80/85 yen in late Tokyo trade. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said persistent rumours that the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was buying dollars helped sustain the rally, in addition to continued selling of the yen by Japanese investors keen to hunt for higher yielding foreign bonds. \par \par \uc2  The fear of Bank of Japan action meant dealers were wary of selling off dollars too aggressively. "Speculation of BOJ (Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  haunted the market and none of us could go short today," said a trust bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Many dealers said the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seemed to have bought dollars during the morning rally at 103.30 yen, but they added that a flurry of purchases by other operators dominated morning activity. \par \par \uc2  Riding a wave of buying from U.S securities firms, overseas fund operators and Japanese investors, the dollar had earlier soared to 103.80 yen, its highest level since June 1994. This was a rise of more than three yen since Tuesday, when the dollar made its decisive leap over the key 100 yen hurdle. The late Tokyo price was slightly higher than the dollar's New York finish of 102.65/70 yen on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "It seems fairly obvious now that the monetary authorities really seem to be trying to push the dollar as far as 105 yen," said Sidney Hui, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at the Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar's energy began to fade as Tokyo trade progressed, with U.S. funds selling dollars heavily to cash in profits from the rally, triggering stop-loss sales. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers also attributed the dollar's easing to comments by Japan's Economic Planning Agency minister Isamu Miyazaki, who told executives of trading firms that wild moves in exchange rates are undesirable. \par \par \uc2  "It would be better for the yen's exchange rate to stay around levels that reflect economic fundamentals without fluctuating wildly," Miyazaki was quoted as saying. \par \par \uc2  "Overseas operators saw it as a sign that authorities do not want a further dollar rise," said a European bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  Many dealers agreed that the dollar's climb to a 15-month high was too sharp, and said the afternoon easing was part of position adjustment ahead of a three-day weekend here. \par \par \uc2  But some dealers did not rule out the possibility of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and added that should it take place during the holiday, the dollar would easily hit 105 yen. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also added that sentiment for the dollar remains bullish as its resurgence has been helped by increased overseas investment by Japanese investors. \par \par \uc2  Deregulatory measures announced by the Japanese government on August 2 are seen as one key factor in re-igniting interest in investing abroad. "The government deregulatory policies and other support initiated the dollar's rise, and then investors joined in," said Yasuhiko Matsunaga, senior manager at the Industrial Bank of Japan (IBJ). \par \par \uc2  Japanese securities houses were seen actively buying Australian dollars and major European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the yen, backed by individual investors' strong interest for higher yielding foreign bonds. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr9e0419o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
