\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Of Japan Bought/103.30 Yen -2: 7-Mo High Vs DEM \b0\par\par\uc2 190 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 1995\par 03:32\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (AP-DJ)--Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday ratcheted the dollar up to the 103.50 yen level in the Tokyo morning, traders said. \par \par \uc2  They said the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought the dollar around and above the 103.30 yen level not long after 0100 GMT. \par \par \uc2  'It doesn't really matter whether they buy dollars or not,' a Tokyo bank trader said. 'I don't think the dollar's in any danger of falling right now anyway.' \par \par \uc2  Around 0130 GMT, the dollar is trading at 103.50 yen and 1.5025 marks, well above 103.08 yen and 1.4972 marks around 0030 GMT. The dollar's level against the mark reflects its highest since Feb. 16, when it reached an intraday high of 1.5092 marks. \par \par \uc2  Traders said even as the dollar has made up long lost ground against the yen, it has lagged in its gains against the mark, and they expect to see the dollar continue to push higher against the German \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 13-09-95 \par \par \uc2  0132GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025dr9e075eq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
