\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar makes headway above 100 yen in Tokyo trade. \b0\par\par\uc2  By George Nishiyama \par \uc2 579 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 12 (Reuter) - The dollar climbed to new heights above 100 yen in Tokyo trade on Tuesday, finally making some headway after previous forays above the psychologically key level turned out to be short-lived. \par \par \uc2  The greenback built on New York gains, moving decisively above 100.00 yen in early Asian trading time and managing to rise as far as 100.60 yen, its highest level in eight months. \par \par \uc2  "The fact that the dollar rebounded in New York despite its lower close in Tokyo trade yesterday showed that its downside is solid, and so dealers are going to test the topside again today," said a dealer with a Japanese trust bank. \par \par \uc2  The 100 yen level appeared to be an easy goal for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Friday, when Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  slashed by half its already record-low key lending rate to 0.5 percent and followed up with aggressive dollar-buying. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar rally had lost steam by Monday as a lack of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by other international monetary authorities led to disappointment in the market. \par \par \uc2  But by Tokyo afternoon trade on Tuesday, the dollar was managing to hold on at around 100.40/50 yen, above the high of 100.20 yen marked last Friday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the rise stemmed largely from mark purchases against the yen, which in turn dragged down the yen versus the dollar. \par \par \uc2  But they also said that despite the inaction by other central banks, fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) was giving underlying support to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ was out in the market for three straight days last week including Friday. That's a sign of its strong will to drive up the dollar," said  Noriyuki Takano, vice president at Chase Manhattan Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers also attributed the dollar's firmness to market talk that a high-ranking finance ministry official, in a meeting with bank dealers on Monday, implied that he sees a target range for the dollar at 105 to 110 yen. \par \par \uc2  Takano also said U.S. funds were buying hefty amounts of marks for yen, giving the dollar support versus the yen. \par \par \uc2  The mark was standing at around 68.10 yen at Tokyo noon, up considerably from its close of 67.85 yen in New York on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also said a good part of the dollar's strength appeared to be the result of buying designed to trigger options-related stop-loss buying at around 100.50 yen. An option gives the holder the right to buy or sell a financial instrument at a fixed price by a certain date. \par \par \uc2  Most dealers said in the near term, the dollar would have to overcome selling pressure from Japanese exporters and profit-taking by dealers keen to cash in on the dollar's moves in order to gain further ground. \par \par \uc2  "Profit-taking interest was strong at around 100.50 yen," a dealer with a U.S. bank in Tokyo said. \par \par \uc2  But some traders also said the dollar may rise further in European and New York trade as overseas operators still had room left to buy the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "While most Japanse operators have already built substantial dollar long positions, overseas players still have slack left," said a dealer with a Swiss bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar will hit 101 yen tonight," he predicted. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr9c03ymn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppic