\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ rate cut fires up Tokyo, Hong Kong bourses. \b0\par\par\uc2  By John O'Callaghan \par \uc2 1,939 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Sept 8 (Reuter) - A historic cut in the Bank of Japan's (BOJ) key lending rate lit a fire under the Tokyo bourse on Friday, but many Asian stock markets were left out in the cold by domestic worries. \par \par \uc2  The dollar breached 100 yen several times in afternoon Tokyo trade after the move and on heavy BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  halved the official discount rate to an all-time low of 0.5 percent to give a jolt to the sluggish economy and take some pressure off the country's beleaguered banking system. \par \par \uc2  "The discount rate cut will help wipe out uncertainties over the future economy and ensure Japan's economic recovery," Finance Minister Masayoshi Takemura told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo stocks leapt in the afternoon on the rate cut and the weaker yen, with many institutional sellers changing tack and semiconductor-related shares commanding heavy interest. \par \par \uc2  The 225-share Nikkei average jumped 658.37 points or 3.74 percent to 18,279.55, its highest close since February 13. No sector fell. \par \par \uc2  "I believe the Nikkei is now on the way to 19,000 and then to 20,000," said Harushige Kobayashi, deputy general manager at Yamaichi Securities. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong stocks rose 1.23 percent as traders bought heavily late in the session amid hopes the U.S. Federal Open Market Committee would follow the BOJ's lead at its meeting on September 26. \par \par \uc2  The blue chip Hang Seng Index jumped 114.22 points or 1.23 percent to 9,393.21. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan shares fended off early profit-taking on rumours the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might further lower bank reserve requirements later in the day, brokers said. The index rose 49.07 points or 0.96 percent to 5,182.51. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore and Bombay bourses also ended slightly higher. Markets in South Korea and Sri Lanka were closed. \par \par \uc2  But Manila's composite index sunk below 2,700 as foreign investors unloaded blue chips on concern over rising interest rates and inflation. It fell 22.68 points or 0.84 percent to 2,688.24. \par \par \uc2  Thai stocks were bogged down all day as investors continued to fret about inflation. The SET Index fell 3.96 points or 0.31 percent to 1,275.68 in thin trade. \par \par \uc2  "The same negative factors are still in place, plus the normal Friday caution," an analyst at Poonpipat Finance and Securities said. \par \par \uc2  Heavy late selling in Fletcher Challenge and Telecom dragged down New Zealand shares but bookseller Whitcoulls went the other way after 11.2 million of its shares were sold. The NZSE-40 index fell 19.34 points or 0.91 percent to 2,105.51. \par \par \uc2  Share prices sagged in Sydney, Kuala Lumpur and Jakarta. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was stronger against the yen and the mark in late Hong Kong trade but faced heavy resistance at 100 yen. Early activity in Europe kept the dollar steady as traders waited to see whether a U.S. rate cut would follow and the BOJ would buy At 1207 GMT, the dollar was at 99.85/90 yen and 1.4872/77 marks, against the local opening of 99.26/31 yen and 1.4814/19 marks and New York's close of 99.13/20 yen and 1.4802/12 marks. \par \par \uc2  Gold closed slightly firmer in Hong Kong in line with silver prices on late short-covering and follow-through buying, dealers said. Bullion ended at US$382.70/383.20 an ounce against New York's $382.60/383.10 close and the local opening of $382.50/383.00. \par \par \uc2  Spot crude prices were little changed in sluggish Asian trade. October 15-day Brent was assessed at $16.65/16.70 as SIMEX Brent traded at $16.67, slightly higher from IPE's $16.62 close on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Markets in South Korea and Sri Lanka were closed on Friday. Following are the market highlights elsewhere in Asia: \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  TOKYO - Tokyo stocks soared in response to an interest rate cut by the Bank of Japan (BOJ) and a further weakening of the yen. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei average shot up 658.37 points or 3.74 percent to 18,279.55 after going as high as 18,501.35. It was the highest close since 18,313.86 on February 13. \par \par \uc2  "After the BOJ cut its discount rate and guided short-term rates lower, and the yen dropped further, it was hard to find reasons to sell shares," a Cosmo Securities trader said. \par \par \uc2  Futures also rocketed, helped by a much higher-than- expected settlement price for September Nikkei 225 futures. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG - Hong Kong stocks were sharply higher on heavy late buying as sentiment improved after Japan cut its discount rate, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The blue chip Hang Seng Index jumped 114.22 points or 1.23 percent to 9,393.21. Turnover was HK$3.59 billion ($463 million). \par \par \uc2  "Traders bought heavily late in the day, probably encouraged by the 3.7 percent surge in Tokyo on the back of Japan's half-point rate cut," a broker with a European house said. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said the market was lifted by hopes that the United States might cut rates after the Federal Open Market Committee meeting on September 26. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY - Share prices rallied to a higher close on the Bombay Stock Exchange (BSE), led by truck maker Tata Engineering and Locomotive Company Ltd (TELCO), brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The 30-share BSE index rose 22.14 points to a provisional 3,366.25. The 100-share National index gained 1.32 points to a provisional 1,539.63. \par \par \uc2  DSP Financial Consultants, the Indian associate of Merrill Lynch, said in a report it forecast TELCO's net profit would grow 98 percent to 6.3 billion rupees ($195 million) in 1995/96 and 43 percent to nine billion rupees ($279 million) the following year. \par \par \uc2  "The market was mostly dull otherwise because there were fewer players," said a trader. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK - Thai stocks ended lower in thin trade with the index trapped in negative territory throughout the session, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The SET Index fell 3.96 points or 0.31 percent to 1,275.68 on small 2.96 billion baht ($118 million) turnover. \par \par \uc2  "The same negative factors are still in place, plus the normal Friday caution. The decline was worsened by sharp setbacks of Siam Cement and PTT Exploration and Production," an analyst at Poonpipat Finance and Securities said. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA - Jakarta share prices were slightly lower in active trading after late profit-taking in SMART and some small-capital stocks, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The Jakarta composite index fell 1.41 points or 0.29 percent to 490.00. \par \par \uc2  Rig Tender was actively traded, continuing to rise on market talk it was likely to win lucrative contracts, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  KUALA LUMPUR - The depressed Malaysian market got another shot of bad news as Aokam Perdana and Land & General (L&G) fell in heavy volume, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The Kuala Lumpur Stock Exchange's Composite Index edged down 1.03 points or 0.10 percent to 982.82. \par \par \uc2  "It's another round of disappointment," said Audrey Ho, associate director at Mohaiyani Securities. \par \par \uc2  Lower forecasts for Aokam's full-year net earnings hurt its shares. Bank Negara's call for banks to show restraint in lending to the upper end of the property market hit L&G, whose new projects target upmarket customers. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  MANILA - Philippine shares ended lower as foreign investors sold blue chips on concerns over rising interest rates and inflation, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The composite index broke its 2,700 support as it fell to 2,688.24, down 22.68 points or 0.84 percent. \par \par \uc2  At Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  auction, the yield on 91-day treasury bills rose by 66.9 basis points to 9.998 percent. \par \par \uc2  Jerome Tan at Guoco Securities said the absence of good news in the market also contributed to the gloomy sentiment. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI - Shanghai B shares were little changed in sluggish trading as investors remained sidelined before the release of details on Pudong preferential policies, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The index edged down 0.022 of a point or 0.04 percent to 61.756 on thin volume of 5.819 million shares. \par \par \uc2  Media reports said Shanghai's municipal government would shortly announce new favourable policies for Pudong, China's hottest development area, but gave no details. \par \par \uc2  Shanghai's A share index lost 2.971 points or 0.38 percent to 779.326 on large turnover of 2.047 billion yuan ($246 million). Brokers said the market was consolidating after heavy gains in the past few days on rises in Pudong shares. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  SHENZHEN - Shenzhen A shares ended narrowly lower on a lack of buying incentives, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The A share index slipped 0.77 of a point to 139.11 as turnover contracted to 267 million yuan ($32 million). \par \par \uc2  The hard-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  B share index shed 0.33 of a point to 73.40. Turnover was HK$9.53 million ($1.23 million). \par \par \uc2  Brokers said a holiday mood before Saturday's mid-autumn festival nudged investors to the sidelines. Trading in B shares is expected to pick up on Monday, when Wuxi Weifu's 68 million B shares will be listed. \par \par \uc2  The composite index retreated 0.74 of a point to 135.29 on total turnover of 441 million yuan ($53 million). \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE - Singapore shares closed higher on a technical bounce but analysts were sceptical about the near-term prospects, saying the market still had ample room to move down. \par \par \uc2  The Straits Times Industrials Index rose 6.45 points or 0.30 percent to 2,129.35. It lost 3.18 points on the week. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think funds will return in a big way," said Dianne Lim, research director at Standard Chartered Securities. \par \par \uc2  Largely disappointing half-year corporate earnings were to blame in part for the lack of institutional demand, she said. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  SYDNEY - The Australian share market was weaker in slow trade as a lack of interest made direction hard to find. \par \par \uc2  The All Ordinaries index inched down three points or 0.14 percent to 2,139.40. \par \par \uc2  The September share price index contract was five points lower at 2,157, a 17.60-point premium to the physical market. \par \par \uc2  Traders said there was a general lack of interest ahead of the weekend, with the weak signals emanating from offshore markets contributing to range-bound and directionless trading. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  TAIPEI - Taiwan share prices closed higher despite early profit-taking on market rumours that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may further lower bank reserve requirements later in the day following Japan's discount rate cut, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The index rose 49.07 points or 0.96 percent to 5,182.51. Turnover was an active T$62.5 billion ($2.3 billion). \par \par \uc2  Some investors and brokers also speculated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may cut the island's rediscount rate, currently at 5.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  "There are market rumours that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will announce cutting bank reserve requirements at this afternoon's news conference," Eric Liu of National Securities said. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  WELLINGTON - Heavy late selling in Fletcher Challenge and Telecom dragged the New Zealand share market to a sharply lower close. \par \par \uc2  The NZSE-40 index fell 19.34 points or 0.91 percent to 2,105.51. \par \par \uc2  But the day's highlight was the sale 11.2 million shares of Whitcoulls -- or 9.2 percent of its stock -- at NZ$2.15 ($1.40) per share, which boosted overall volume and the bookseller's price 24 cents to NZ$2.20 ($1.43). \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr980442n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
