\par\par \b \uc2  Japan cuts key interest rate, dollar hits 100 yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Linda Sieg \par \uc2 658 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 8 (Reuter) - Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  slashed its key lending rate by half on Friday to a new all-time low of 0.5 percent to bolster the country's flagging economy. \par \par \uc2  Within two hours of the cut in the official discount rate, the dollar reached the 100-yen level for the first time since January. The rise was helped by aggressive dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Tokyo stock market's Nikkei average also soared, rising more than four percent to 18,442.39 by mid-afternoon. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan last cut the key lending rate to its previous record low of one percent in April. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also said it was twinning the cut in the key lending rate with a move to guide short-term rates lower in the money market. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of Japan expects that these decisions will lead to a further decline in interest rates across the board, thereby contributing to a steady recovery of the economy with price stability," it said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  In Washington, U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin quickly welcomed the BOJ move, and reaffirmed the U.S. commitment to cooperate closely in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets with Japan and other leading industrial nations. \par \par \uc2  "We welcome the actions taken by the Japanese authorities to ease monetary conditions," Rubin said in a statement. "These actions which are consistent with the objectives of the April 25 G7 (Grup of Seven) communique, should help contribute to conditions that would promote a recovery in domestic demand (in Japan), greater financial stability and a continuation of recent developments in exchange markets." \par \par \uc2  Analysts -- who had expected the move but thought it might not come so soon -- had said lower interest rates were needed to kick-start the economy, keep the yen on a downward track and provide relief to Japan's troubled banking sector. \par \par \uc2  Japanese banks and other institutions are struggling under bad debts inherited from the collapse of sky-high land and share prices in the early 1990s. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the cut in the key rate would help get the economy back on track and provide relief to the financial institutions by improving profit margins. \par \par \uc2  "On the monetary side we have a full commitment to reflate -- these are decisive steps to try to kick-start the economy," said Jesper Koll, chief economist at JP Morgan in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "By aggressively cutting interest rates they are helping the financial system because of lower funding costs," he said. \par \par \uc2  "What you're going to see is the positive impact from monetary policy over the next 18 months," said Russell Jones, chief economist at Lehman Brothers Japan. "I'm quite optimistic about the ability of the economy to recover." \par \par \uc2  But the credit easing will hardly solve all of Japan's economic problems, many of which are structural rather than merely the result of a cyclical downturn, economists said. \par \par \uc2  "Of course it helps...it's necessary to ensure that the economy makes progress rather than regresses," said Chris Calderwood, economist at Barclays de Zoete Wedd in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "It's necessary but not sufficient," he said. \par \par \uc2  Economists said it should followed by hefty government spending to boost domestic demand. "We have to see how they perform on the fiscal side and see whether we get similar decisive measures," Koll said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese leaders have pledged to unveil around September 20 a package of steps to stimulate the economy, to be backed by a hefty extra budget for the business year to March 31, 1996. \par \par \uc2  Several economists said the rate could could well be the last, but some were reluctant to rule out further credit easings completely. \par \par \uc2  "Until it gets down to zero, it's hard to be emphatic but certainly when the rate is so low there is a much better prospect that its the last cut," Calderwood said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr98043y8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
