\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Japan cuts key rate to lift flagging economy. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Linda Sieg \par \uc2 678 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 8 (Reuter) - Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  slashed its key lending rate by half on Friday to an historic low of 0.5 percent to bolster the country's flagging economy and give relief to its fragile banking system. \par \par \uc2  The move, backed by aggressive dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sent the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaping briefly above the 100 yen level for the first time since January, and pushed Tokyo share prices up by nearly four percent. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which last cut its discount rate in April, to the previous record low of one percent, said it was twinning the cut with a move to guide short-term market rates lower by providing ample funds to the money market. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of Japan expects these decisions will lead to a further decline in interest rates across the board, thereby contributing to a steady recovery in the economy with price stability," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masayoshi Takemura also said the rate cut would help the economy, which has been trapped at near zero growth for nearly four years. \par \par \uc2  The index, which compares the number of firms seeing worse business conditions ahead with the number expecting improvement, is expected to stay stuck at minus 18 in November, when the next survey takes place. \par \par \uc2  Japanese big business applauded the rate cut, as did the United States, where Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin issued a statement saying he welcomed the easing and confirming the U.S. commitment to international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market cooperation. \par \par \uc2  "The discount rate cut will help wipe out uncertainties over the future economy and ensure Japan's economic recovery," Takemura told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  Clearly, the uncertainties are great. \par \par \uc2  A Bank of Japan survey, released after the rate cut was announced, showed Japanese corporate executives became gloomier about business prospects in August despite a weaker yen. \par \par \uc2  A key component of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's survey, the diffusion index for major manufacturers, fell to minus 18 in August from minus 16 in May, when the survey was last conducted -- the first time companies' outlook had worsened since November 1993. \par \par \uc2  Analysts had said lower interest rates were needed to kick-start the economy and give relief to Japan's troubled banking sector, struggling under bad debt inherited from the collapse of sky-high land and share prices in the early 1990s. \par \par \uc2  The cut in the key rate, at which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lends to other banks, will help get the economy back on track and help financial firms by improving profit margins, economists said. \par \par \uc2  "On the monetary side we have a full commitment to reflate -- these are decisive steps to try to kick-start the economy," said Jesper Koll, chief economist at JP Morgan in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  But the credit easing will hardly solve all of Japan's economic problems, many of which are structural rather than merely the result of a cyclical downturn, economists said. \par \par \uc2  "Of course it helps...it's necessary to ensure that the economy makes progress rather than regresses," said Chris Calderwood, an economist at Barclays de Zoete Wedd in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "It's necessary but not sufficient," he said. \par \par \uc2  Lower rates need to be backed up by hefty government spending to boost domestic demand and by deregulation to cut the red tape that strangles enterprise, economists said. "We have to see how they perform on the fiscal side and see whether we get similar decisive measures," said JP Morgan's Koll. \par \par \uc2  Japanese leaders have pledged to unveil around September 20 a package of steps to stimulate the economy, to be backed by a hefty extra budget for the business year to next March 31. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the rate cut -- the ninth since 1991 -- could well be the last. \par \par \uc2  "Until it gets down to zero, it's hard to be emphatic, but certainly when the rate is so low there is a much better prospect that it's the last cut," BZW's Calderwood said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr98043vi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
