\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar touches 100 yen level after Japan rate cut. \b0\par\par\uc2 255 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 8 (Reuter) - The dollar rose to the key 100 yen level in Tokyo trade on Friday, achieving heights not seen in more than seven months, after surging on the back of a cut in Japan's official discount rate. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo brokers said the dollar touched exactly 100.00 yen after the rate cut and aggressive dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it later fell back slightly to trade just below that level. \par \par \uc2  Earlier the Bank of Japan (BOJ) cut its key official discount rate in half to an historic low of 0.5 percent to bolster the flagging economy. It also said it would guide money market rates lower to increase the impact. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  swiftly entered the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and carried out aggressive dollar-buying to push up the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "The BOJ has been bidding up dollars aggressively to drive it up above 100 yen," said a European bank trader. \par \par \uc2  The dollar then slipped to trade just under 100 yen, at around 99.84/89 yen, in afternoon Tokyo trade. Share prices also soared on the rate cut, with the Tokyo market's key Nikkei average rising more than four percent to 18,404.69 by mid-afternoon. \par \par \uc2  In Washington, U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin quickly welcomed Japan's decision to cut interest rates, and reaffirmed the U.S. commitment to cooperate closely in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets with its Group of Seven allies. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr98043pq\par }\page {\pa