\par\par \b \uc2  Japan markets divided on credit-easing scenario. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Risa Maeda \par \uc2 627 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 7 (Reuter) - Japan's financial markets have little doubt the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will soon lower interest rates. They are just divided on whether it will do this by cutting its key lending rate or via money market manouevres. \par \par \uc2  Money traders said on Thursday the most likely scenario is that the Bank of Japan will cut its key discount rate by as much as 0.5 percentage point in late September, to coincide with a government package of economic stimulus measures. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last cut the discount rate, which it charges on its loans to other banks, in April to a record low of 1.00 percent. \par \par \uc2  But many traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may choose an alternative -- guiding market interest rates lower by supplying more funds to the money market, which was what it chose to do starting on July 7. \par \par \uc2  "There is the possibility of a discount rate cut if the BOJ (Bank of Japan) gives in to political pressure and decides to cut the rate to support the government's fiscal measures," said Yoji Iwasaki, senior money market analyst at Asahi Bank. \par \par \uc2  "But the bank still has time to consider whether a rate cut is really essential in boosting the economy," he added. \par \par \uc2  Another cut in this key rate may actually hurt Japanese financial institutions, many of which are already labouring under huge bad loans. \par \par \uc2  A discount rate cut would eventually affect all interest rates. But rates on bank deposits fall more slowly than those in the money market, which means the banks would have to keep paying high interest to depositors while the interest they receive from borrowers drops, money dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Total operating profits at Japan's 11 major city banks for the half-year to September 30 are likely rise 23 percent to a record of 1.34 trillion yen ($13.6 billion), Japan's leading financial daily Nippon Keizai Shimbun reported on Thursday. It said this was mainly because the BOJ's policy of lowering market rates has enabled banks to raise money at very low cost. \par \par \uc2  "Keeping money rates low will help major banks decrease their bad loans, although not all financial institutions will be able to get rid of theirs without other types of government support," a trust bank money dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Asahi's Iwasaki said: "If the BOJ cut the discount rate, it would have fewer cards to use for another economic crisis, which could still happen if the U.S. stops cooperating with Japan (on monetary policy), or the Nikkei (stock average) falls below 14,000 or the dollar falls to 70 yen." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was salvaged from historically low levels against the yen, which it hit in April, when the United States and Japan lowered interest rates in July. To enhance this, the two nations conducted dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted restrictions on overseas investments to induce dollar buying. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has recovered to nearly 99 yen from its low of 79.75 yen hit in April. This turnaround in the dollar also buoyed the Tokyo stock market. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile Shoji Kitta, managing director of major money broking house Tokyo Tanshi, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could lower the discount rate while also supplying ample funds to the money market. The BOJ is likely to cut its discount rate by 0.25 point around the time the government unveils its economic package, he said. \par \par \uc2  "If the BOJ cut the rate by 0.5 point, bond yields may rebound (and bond prices drop) on views that the discount rate has hit bottom, with the government package expected to require deficit-financing bond issues," he said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr9704ai9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
