\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar surges more than full yen in Tokyo trade. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Elaine Lies \par \uc2 544 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 6 (Reuter) - The dollar surged more than one full yen in Tokyo trade on Wednesday, rising above 99 yen on waves of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar buying and bringing the elusive 100 yen level suddenly back into view. \par \par \uc2  The first round of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) came hard on the heels of the release of Japanese trade figures, which showed the nation's current account surplus shrank in July. \par \par \uc2  Although the figures themselves failed to galvanise the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the surprise \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action did the trick, sending the dollar sprinting higher. \par \par \uc2  "The fact that the BOJ timed its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to coincide with the release of the current account data was probably to show the market it wants to raise the dollar even higher, possibly even as far as 100 yen," said Hirokazu Note, a manager at Sumitomo Bank. \par \par \uc2  Japan's surplus in the current account -- the broadest measure of trade in goods and services -- was $9.22 billion in July, down from a revised $11.60 billion a year earlier, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) data released early Wednesday morning. \par \par \uc2  Previously, the monthly data was released during the afternoon in Tokyo, but the ministry brought the release time forward to conform with the practice in other countries, which issue economic data before financial markets open in their area. \par \par \uc2  Economists have said the authorities wanted to shift the time because Japan's stubborn surpluses -- a key cause of yen strength -- are at last shrinking and they hoped the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market would greet the data by buying dollars and selling yen. \par \par \uc2  A senior BOJ official told Reuters that the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was moving positively in response to the fall in the surplus, adding that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  welcomed the fact that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were moving in line with economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  But most dealers said the market shrugged off the data and its lukewarm response may perhaps even have triggered the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The market basically ignored the (current account) data which was largely in line with market expectations. Perhaps MOF and the BOJ did not like the fact that the market ignored it," one dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "The current account figures themselves didn't really provoke much of a response. It was the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly after, which was unexpected, that did it. The surprise made it very effective," Sumitomo's Note said. \par \par \uc2  Successive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and purchases by U.S. funds fed the buying frenzy, pushing the dollar as high as 99.12 yen and 1.4712 marks before sales from players eager to take profits kicked in, forcing the greenback to retreat slightly. In late afternoon trade the dollar stood at 99.00/10 yen and 1.4752/62 marks, against a New York close of 97.65/75 yen and 1.4620/25 marks. \par \par \uc2  Although the dollar touched a six-month high of 99.40 yen last week, the final climb to 100 yen is proving more arduous than expected, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "People are still rather wary about hanging on to positions above 99 yen and are therefore very quick to sell," a British bank dealer added. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr9604901\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
