\par\par \b \uc2  JGBs end Tokyo firmer on light short-covering. \b0\par\par\uc2 462 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 6 (Reuter) - Key December Japanese government bond (JGB) futures closed firmer as day-traders, who went short on the yen's fall this morning, bought back later in the day. \par \par \uc2  The dollar briefly rose as high as 99.12 yen after reports of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's intermittent dollar buying, compared with its New York close of 97.65/75 yen on Tuesday. It stayed below 99.00 yen for most of the afternoon session. \par \par \uc2  "Hopes of a credit easing remained, but buying was curbed at some point due to a lack of participation from investors," said a senior trader at bank's securities arm. \par \par \uc2  The December ended up 0.10 at 118.10. \par \par \uc2  The contract rose to 118.31 soon after the opening. It then fell as low as 117.93 as the yen was dampened by large-scale dollar buying by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ). \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was very powerful, and it was a bit surprising," a trust bank dealer said, adding that this was the main reason for the yen bonds' sharp fall in late morning trade. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said investors were absent ahead of half-year book closings, and this left the market at a loss. "Futures dived, but cash didn't follow. Buying on dips didn't emerge, either. That's because investors were away," said a bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  The market was sensitive to any news raising the possibility of a BOJ credit easing after rallying aggressively on trade minister Ryutaro Hashimoto's comments on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Hashimoto called for "every possible step" to boost the nation's flagging economy, remarks which increased market hopes of a cut in the BOJ's key official discount rate. \par \par \uc2  But operators were also nervous about possible oversupply from increasing domestic straight bond issues, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Medium-term bond yields are getting less attractive," said the senior trader. Kawasaki Heavy Industries Ltd launched four- and five-year bonds of 10 billion yen each today. \par \par \uc2  NEC Corp also launched five-, seven- and 10-year bonds in volumes of 20, 40 and 10 billion yen, respectively. \par \par \uc2  Several other bonds are expected to be launched this month. Volumes may total nearly 500 billion yen in September. \par \par \uc2  Many operators will watch the BOJ's "Tankan" corporate survey due Friday for a clue to changes in the bank's monetary policy, dealers said. "The market will react more if the data shows the economy improving rather than stalled," said one. \par \par \uc2  The key 174th 10-year JGB yielded 3.015 percent at 0800 GMT against Tuesday's 2.980 close. It moved within a range of 2.960 and 3.015. -- Risa Maeda, Tokyo Treasury Desk (81-334328570) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr96048za\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
