\par\par \b \uc2  Central banks due for repeat performance on dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrew Young \par \uc2 648 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 31 (Reuter) - The big three central banks will probably show their hand again in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in the near future amid doubts recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has secured a strong enough footing for the dollar, said economists. \par \par \uc2  Although it is generally agreed the dollar-buying spree by the U.S., Japan and Germany on August 15 was well timed and provided a short-term boost for the U.S. unit, key upside barriers still remain unbroken, they noted. \par \par \uc2  The Group of Three (G3) unofficial target for the dollar is thought to be at around 1.50 marks and 100 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has so far failed to launch a serious attack on these levels despite the threat of further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a recent aggressive cut in German rates and fresh worries over the Japanese financial system. \par \par \uc2  However, the recent corrective move lower by the dollar after a push above 99.00 yen on Wednesday probably rules out a round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the moment unless it seriously threatens the lower levels, said economists. \par \par \uc2  "I can't really see the central banks coming in at the moment. They would prefer to come in when the dollar is going up," said Chris Turner, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BZW here. \par \par \uc2  "But I would expect the Japanese are still keen to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and I would not be surprised if the Bundesbank also came in at some stage," he added. \par \par \uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks a good bet given the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aim to weaken the yen and the necessity to make a large injection of yen into the money markets via \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid mounting Japanese liquidity problems. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's recent stab above 99.00 yen was prompted by worries about the Japanese financial system following the collapse on Wednesday of one bank and the partial suspension of Japan's largest credit union, Kizu Shinyo Kumiai. \par \par \uc2  Speculation of a near-term cut in the Japanese ODR rate and the belief Japan will continue to announce measures to undermine the yen should provide a solid floor for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "It's virtually inevitable the authorities come back and push dollar/yen up through 100 and ultimately the year's high at 101.50 -- it's just at question of time," said Steve Hannah, director of research at IBJ International here. \par \par \uc2  Tony Norfield, economist at ABN AMRO, said now is an ideal time for the central banks to catapult the dollar through 100 as, technically, the picture then points to further sharp gains. \par \par \uc2  Hannah at IBJ said the U.S., Japan and Germany are clearly committed to avoiding financial meltdown in Japan. But he said they may want to wait for dollar-positive, rather than yen-negative news before a fresh bout of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Economists said another factor reinforcing the view that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely is the belief by some that the Bundesbank wants the dollar even higher than the 1.50 marks level. \par \par \uc2  "Why would the Bundesbank buy the dollar when it was already rising in the mid-1.40s (on August 15) if it wanted it to go to 1.50 and no more?," said BZW's Turner. \par \par \uc2  "We believe the Bundesbank's preferred range for dollar/mark is a broad 1.50 to 1.65," he added. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the annual average of dollar/mark throughout 1990-94 was between 1.56 to 1.66, and probably reflects a more desirable range for the Bundesbank. \par \par \uc2  "The real German economy is now struggling," said Adrian Cunningham, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at UBS in London. \par \par \uc2  "So I don't think the Bundesbank would be unduly concerned if you saw dollar/mark at the mid 1.50s or even 1.60, as long as monetary conditions are sufficiently sound as not to undermine the disinflationary outlook," he added. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr8v04g6y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
