\par\par \b \uc2  Tokyo dealers cool to options as  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  tool. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tatsuo Ito \par \uc2 476 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 August 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 23 (Reuter) - Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said they were doubtful \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary authorities would start using options as an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tool in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  "I think the possibility is basically low, given that the other major industrial nations never use it," a senior options dealer at a European bank said. \par \par \uc2  Japan's monetary authorities have conducted a long study of the options markets, including \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  using options, a finance ministry official told Reuters recently. \par \par \uc2  "There are no central banks that don't study them since the option markets are firmly established as an extension of the futures and spot markets," the official said. \par \par \uc2  But he said it was not clear if options \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would actually materialise. \par \par \uc2  He said the U.S. Federal Reserve had examined the options markets and there was talk that Spain's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had already used them for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they were aware Tokyo authorities had been studying options for over a year, but added that they still doubted the feasibility and effect of such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "If the authorities really conduct it, there would be a great psychological impact on the market. But probably that's all," said the dealer at the European bank. \par \par \uc2  Although conventional \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market succeeded in lifting the dollar against the yen during the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent rallies, buying in a downward spiral earlier this year proved less effective in global markets, where about $1 trillion is traded every day. \par \par \uc2  Leveraged \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have a greater impact on exchange rates, but at the same time carries unlimited risks, especially in the selling of put options, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The first question is whether the monetary authorites will buy or sell options," said one strategist. \par \par \uc2  If they buy dollar call options in a bid to push the dollar higher with a strike price such as 105 yen, that would induce additional dollar buying by banks which underwrite the options to them as the dollar keeps rising. \par \par \uc2  But such options might present problems because setting a strike price could show to which levels the authorities intend to guide the dollar/yen rate, something they have been loath to reveal. \par \par \uc2  Obligated dollar buying through put options may carry risks if the dollar keeps falling, but also may be effective in offsetting options activity by Japanese exporters, which was seen earlier this year and aggravated the dollar's dive, options traders said. \par \par \uc2  "Options \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is possible, notably for correcting the distortion of the options market which also affects the spot market condition," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers dismissed the possibility of a new type of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of hand, saying the authorities were simply trying to hint they still held cards to redress the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0