\par\par \b \uc2  Size of fall in Japan surplus stuns economists. \b0\par\par\uc2 482 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 August 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 15 (Reuter) - The size of a fall in Japan's trade surplus in July came as a surprise and helped reinforce a view that it is on a gradual declining trend, economists said. \par \par \uc2  "I was very, very surprised because it (the trade surplus) was exactly at the lowest forecast," said Makoto Kojima, director of global markets at Barclays Bank. \par \par \uc2  Takumi Tsunoda, an economist at Yamaichi Research Institute of Securities and Economics Inc, said: "Japan's trade surplus is on a declining trend, with the effect of the yen's appreciation starting to depress the volume of exported products." \par \par \uc2  Many economists agreed that this year's rise in the yen against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially the dollar, has weakened Japanese products' competitiveness overseas. \par \par \uc2  Japan's overall customs-cleared trade surplus narrowed to $9.43 billion in July from $12.25 billion a year earlier, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) said. \par \par \uc2  July's overall trade surplus was just below the bottom of forecasts, which ranged from $9.5 billion to $12.2 billion. \par \par \uc2  But Japan's exports may not continue falling sharply because other countries may keep buying Japanese products as their economies are experiencing recovery, economists said. \par \par \uc2  Asian countries, for example, are expected to purchase capital goods from Japan despite the fact that the yen's appreciation boosted prices, said Yamaichi's Tsunoda. \par \par \uc2  Economists said Japan's exports to the United States are also unlikely to continue shrinking dramatically in the future, although the July figure of $10.34 billion marked the first fall in 32 months. \par \par \uc2  The MOF said Japan's trade surplus with the U.S. marked the second consecutive fall in July, down 31.1 percent to $3.87 billion from $5.62 billion a year earlier. \par \par \uc2  The finance ministry official attributed a fall in exports to the U.S. in particular mainly to a decline in shipments of Japanese cars. "The high yen is making Japanese cars less price-competitive," he said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese carmakers are stepping up local production in the United States and car demand there is sluggish, he added. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Japan's imports are expected to rise in terms of both volume and value due to the yen's rise, said an economist at Daiwa Bank Research Institute, who also said the fall in the July trade surplus was unexpectedly sharp. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the size of the fall in the surplus was an incentive for buying dollars against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said falls in the surplus, the strong rally in the Tokyo stock market and a series of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) gave traders the impetus to buy dollars in late Tuesday trade. \par \par \uc2  At 0800 GMT, the dollar was trading just below the psychologically important 95 yen level, rising from around 93.70 yen at 0300 GMT. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr8f04xk1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
