\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar rockets in Tokyo, further gains likely. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Elaine Lies \par \uc2 643 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 11 (Reuter) - The dollar rocketed to a five-month high against the yen and surged against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday, powered by a mix of political and technical factors, and dealers said more gains lay ahead. \par \par \uc2  The greenback surged above the key 93 yen barrier and overcame resistance at 1.4250 marks in Tokyo trade, propelled by heavy bids from overseas operators that helped the dollar accelerate its recent upward swing. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese government welcomed the yen's renewed retreat, with trade minister Ryutaro Hashimoto telling reporters he wanted the dollar to make up still more ground on the yen. \par \par \uc2  By midday the dollar had hit 93.80 yen, its highest level since March 6, before falling back slightly on profit-taking and exporter sales. It also rose as far as 1.4320 marks, the highest against the German \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since June 1. At 0348 GMT it stood at 93.45/55 yen and 1.4283/88 marks. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also jumped against the Swiss franc, touching a high of around 1.1882 against a New York close of 1.1760/70. \par \par \uc2  Although the dollar has risen sharply against the yen over the past few days, its weakness against the mark and other European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had kept a lid on gains. But dealers said Friday's move seemed to signal a genuine upward trend. \par \par \uc2  "You can definitely say today's dollar is strong across the board, and...it will probably rise very fast," said Tomio Kudo, chief customer dealer at Dresdner Bank AG in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "Five months ago, the dollar fell very suddenly from around 96 yen to 91 yen with no resistance. This created a vacuum through which it may rise even faster than we expect," echoed Mitsuru Saito, deputy general manager at Sanwa Bank Ltd. \par \par \uc2  Last week's surprise \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  deregulation package to promote overseas investment, combined with joint dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the United States and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , appeared to be taking effect, with Japanese life insurers and other investors increasing overseas holdings, he added. \par \par \uc2  Heavy dollar buying by Japanese trust banks overnight appeared to support this, although the dollar's gains against the mark were largely the result of buying by U.S. funds. \par \par \uc2  One factor behind the dollar's sudden strength appeared to be fresh instability in the Middle East and the continuing crisis in Bosnia, Dresdner's Kudo said. \par \par \uc2  The Jordanian government said on Thursday it had given refuge to two of Saddam Hussein's sons-in-law, including the mastermind of Iraq's secret military programme, and President Bill Clinton assured Jordan that it could count on U.S. support should Iraq retaliate. \par \par \uc2  Although the dollar had lost much of its traditional status as a "safe-haven" in times of political instability, recent U.S. economic strength seems to have reversed this trend, Kudo said. "The U.S. economy is firm and the government is fairly stable as well, certainly more than Japan's. This is reviving the dollar's attractiveness as a safe haven," he added. \par \par \uc2  Comments on Thursday from U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin that a strong dollar is in the best U.S. interest recalled similar statements made by Japanese officials when the dollar climbed back to the 90 yen level last week, increasing market perceptions that both countries are sincere in maintaining a strong dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Technical factors also fuelled the dollar's gains. "The combination of all these things has been very good for the dollar. And I now see little in the way of a fairly quick rise to 95.00 yen and 1.4500 marks," said Dresdner's Kudo. \par \par \uc2  Trade minister Hashimoto said he wanted the dollar to head back above 100 yen to help the profitability of Japanese firms. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr8b053u6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
