\par\par \b \uc2  Japan investment seen key to lasting dlr recovery. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Anissa Wells \par \uc2 446 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Aug 4 (Reuter) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has applauded Tokyo's move to promote investment, but many experts are beginning to wonder just how much Japanese capital will flow to America's shores and if it will bring a lasting recovery for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "We will see inflows but I don't think they will be large or be able to overcome reluctance on the part of some people to  become dollar bulls and start buying," said Kevin Harris, international economist at MCM \b \highlight22\uc2 CurrencyWatch\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced on Wednesday a series of measures aimed at containing the yen. \par \par \uc2  The actions included removing limits on insurance companies' participation in syndicated loans and on their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans to overseas borrowers. \par \par \uc2  Also announced were steps to promote the flow of government funds abroad. \par \par \uc2  The news, along with a bout of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Federal Reserve, brought the dollar above 91 yen, a level not seen since March. \par \par \uc2  Although the dollar continues to look quite healthy, analysts are wondering if it can sustain the gains without a much-needed infusion of Japanese funds. \par \par \uc2  "Unless there are private flows that come in behind this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the move by the Japanese government, dollar/yen will come off in the next couple of weeks or so," said David Gilmore, partner at the New York-based \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics consulting firm. \par \par \uc2  Many analysts said the Japanese, who have suffered major losses in U.S. markets, will likely be wary of venturing back into dollar-based assets. \par \par \uc2  Ron Leven, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at J.P. Morgan and Co in New York, pointed out that, "The main reason Japanese insurance companies were not going overseas was not because of regulatory constraints. It was because they were cautious and were not happy with the risk/reward in overseas markets." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said they were awaiting next week's Treasury sale of $42.5 billion in new securities to determine whether Japanese investors have had a change of heart. \par \par \uc2  "That will be a first test case," said Scott Pardee, senior advisor at Yamaichi International (America) Inc. \par \par \uc2  "But I think they are going to be cautious about taking on new risks and coming back into the dollar," he added. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, many analysts, like Ashwin Vasan, vice president for global fixed income at Oppenheimer Management Corp, said Japanese investors will likely set their sights on core European markets, particularly Germany, which offer higher yields with minimal \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk. \par \par \uc2  "But, time will tell," added Vasan. "We will know more in the coming months." \par \par \uc2  -- 212-859-1662 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr84055fd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
