\par\par \b \uc2  US-Japan dlr support firm, outcome unsure - dealers. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Elaine Lies \par \uc2 596 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 3 (Reuter) - Japan and the United States have shown renewed determination to cooperate in boosting the dollar, but it is not yet clear that Wednesday's teamwork alone will assure the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's firm recovery, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. and Japan are both concerned about keeping the dollar from harm. But until there's a bit more change, it will be very difficult for the dollar to gain substantially," said Yasuji Yamanaka, a chief dealer at the Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  "Until the dollar makes it to at least 93 yen, I wouldn't say that trends have turned around," another dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "There will be a lot of small adjustments until then." \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 90.70/73 yen at 0455 GMT in Tokyo, pulled down from its overnight high of 91.25 by profit-taking. \par \par \uc2  Although the two countries have previously worked together to strengthen the dollar, most recently in a joint July 7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , this time was different, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  They cited \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Wednesday morning announcement of a deregulatory package designed to encourage domestic operators to invest abroad, which helped the dollar power above the key 90 yen resistance level during Asian time -- gains that were then extended in New York by the joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "(The governments) must have cooperated behind the scenes. If the U.S. hadn't done anything after the announcement of the Japanese package, it just would have been the same old (unsuccessful) pattern," said Yamanaka. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin, hailing the package as "removing impediments to the capital markets", said it was "consistent" with an April 25 G-7 communique in which the G7 agreed to seek an orderly reversal of the dollar's decline. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masayoshi Takemura said in a late Wednesday statement that Japan will continue coordinating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market policy with other major nations. \par \par \uc2  Separately, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official told Reuters on Thursday that Wednesday's concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.S. demonstrated that the G7 rich nations' consensus on an orderly dollar reversal is still intact. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo dealers agreed. "The spirit of coordinated action is, I think, sincere, otherwise the dollar would not have risen so much," said Takako Masai, a dealer at Toronto Dominion Bank. \par \par \uc2  Although dealers said the Japanese package could help the dollar achieve sustained gains, they warned that its impact would depend on how much investment actually resulted. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of investment could eventually even lead to a reduction in Japan's trade surplus, which would also help the dollar firm. But the question is: how much are people actually willing to invest?" said Kazuya Imai, a manager at Hokkaido Takushoku Bank. \par \par \uc2  A key test of Japanese investors' appetite for foreign assets comes next week, when $42.5 billion of U.S. Treasury bonds will be auctioned in the quarterly refunding. \par \par \uc2  "With such a large issue, the U.S. is worried about oversupply, and that bad results here could lead to a 'triple cheapening' of bonds, stocks and the dollar," Imai said. \par \par \uc2  You could even perhaps say this may have prompted the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Imai added. \par \par \uc2  Bank of America's Yamanaka agreed. "The quarterly refunding is an important test. But Japanese investors seem hesitant about buying U.S. bonds, wondering if the bond market is really stable, and at this point it's hard to see what they will do. \par \par \uc2  "The stage is ready. Now all depends on doing it or not," he added. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr8305c42\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
