\par\par \b \uc2  =Japan's Moves To Cap Yen Spur Minor Lift In Dlr Sentiment \b0\par\par\uc2  By LAURA COHN \par \uc2 347 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 1995\par 20:23\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (AP-DJ)--Japan's two-punch attack on the yen propelled the dollar nearly to five-month highs Wednesday, but analysts say the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably has enjoyed the majority of its gains. Concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Federal Reserve and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , along with a new plan from the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , appears to have triggered a minor, more bullish shift in dollar sentiment, analysts say. However, additional dollar buyers will be needed for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sustain its gains, analysts warn. Wednesday, the Japanese MOF announced a package of measures to promote overseas investment and lending by Japanese investors to stem the yen's strength. There were three key parts to the plan, according to economists at High Frequency Economics, a New York consulting firm. The MOF said it will buy more foreign bonds for its trust fund, encourage Japanese life insurers to buy more foreign bonds, and loosen the regulations under which foreign investors borrow yen. \par \par \uc2  "What's happened over the last 24 hours is the first step in what will lessen demand for yen," says Robert Stein, a Chicago-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  advisor. However, Stein emphasizes, questions remain about whether the MOF's new plan will be enough to kick the dollar into a new, higher trend. One of the reasons the dollar got so much mileage out of the moves was that the MOF's plan gave cautious dollar bulls a fundamental reason to buy dollars, Stein explains. Bullish dollar sentiment has been building up over the past several months, as technical traders began to argue that the dollar had bottomed, he adds. Chris Iggo, international economist at Chase Manhattan Bank, agrees that Wednesday's developments are likely to have a short-term impact. "In the near term, this will certainly change sentiment a bit. But whether it actually will lead to shifts in capital flows is another question," he says. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) DOW JONES NEWS 08-02-95 \par \par \uc2  1423EDT \par \par \uc2  4077 4160 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020011024dr8201u53\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
