\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar gives up Tokyo gains on IMF meeting news. \b0\par\par\uc2 498 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juli 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 25 (Reuter) - The dollar gave up its early Tokyo gains on waves of long liquidation triggered by news the U.S. sharply criticised Japan at a secret IMF meeting, arguing that Tokyo was to blame for the strong yen that has hurt its economy and urging it to do more to revive growth. \par \par \uc2  "The news caught Tokyo traders off guard, triggering (long) position squaring," said a local bank trader. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, the dollar gained steadily in Tokyo, helped by purchases by banks and corporate traders on top of persistent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ). \par \par \uc2  "It appears more and more people here have come to cherish the idea of dollar's quick recovery to above 90 yen," said Takashi Okura, chief trader at First National Bank of Chicago. \par \par \uc2  The idea was based on market belief that the U.S. and Japan will cooperate to implement an "orderly reversal of the dollar" as mentioned at the Group of Seven meeting in April. \par \par \uc2  The news of the IMF meeting forced many to reassess their prospects for the dollar, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "The market has been drifting in relatively stable waters. So I don't think traders are willing to put 'U.S.-Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cooperation' to a major test by driving the dollar down," said Okura of First Chicago. \par \par \uc2  International monetary sources said in Washington that the U.S. had made clear at the IMF meeting that it believes the yen's rise against the dollar is a Japanese problem and that its solution lies in Tokyo, not in Washington. The remarks simply underline the long-standing U.S. position of urging Japan to stimulate growth, but do not necessarily crack down on recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cooperation, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, some dealers said persistent dollar buying by the BOJ is delaying the natural recovery of the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ has been trying to tighten up the dollar's supply in the global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets," said a senior local bank trader. \par \par \uc2  But such artificial supportive moves are like morphine shots, and will eventually deprive the market of its natural power of recovery, he added. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in the market, a bank dealer in Singapore said there was good interest in buying the mark at 63.15 yen. But there were large stop-loss selling orders at 62.80 yen. \par \par \uc2  There were rumours that a Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  investor sold the dollar at 88 yen and a Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  investor also sold at the upper range of 87-88 yen. \par \par \uc2  U.S. funds were seen to have sold dollars at 1.3855/60 marks, said traders in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "The tone is slightly bearish and the dollar has to break key support at 1.3820 marks to get going," said a Japanese bank dealer. Stop loss sell orders are seen at 1.3780 marks, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Doreen Siow, Singapore 65-8703092 \par \par \uc2  Yoshiko Mori, Tokyo Treasury Desk 813-3432-8676 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr7p05jqy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
