\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar in doldrums as 90 yen goal proves elusive. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Elaine Lies \par \uc2 697 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juli 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 25 (Reuter) - Japan's pursuit of a 90 yen dollar has seen its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying dollars on a nearly daily basis, but the efforts have been stymied by the market's summer lull, dealers said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  With the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the seasonal doldrums, there are few positive factors to warrant dollar-buying, they said. \par \par \uc2  "As far as Japan is concerned, they want the yen to weaken more to help the economy," said Sidney Hui, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at the Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  "But (while) there's nothing to make the dollar come down, there's no reason for it to rise." \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at around 87.40/45 yen at 0634 GMT, although it was earlier boosted from Monday's New York close of 87.45/55, mainly by Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But it remained substantially below the 3-1/2 month high of 89.27 yen struck on July 17, the closest it has come to the 90 yen level that government sources told Reuters last week was the Japanese government's near-term goal for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "There's a lot of talk about the BOJ wanting to see the dollar above 90-95 yen, maybe even 100, and making 90 yen a psychological support (level)," said Bank of America's Hui. \par \par \uc2  But he said selling interest remained strong above 90 yen, particularly from Asian central banks. Selling by an Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was largely responsible for a quick plunge in the dollar of more than one yen during Monday morning trade. \par \par \uc2  While other dealers agreed that the BOJ's efforts alone were not sufficient to push the dollar higher, they were at a loss to name other sources of strength for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the U.S. Federal Reserve and the BOJ on July 7 proved moderately successful in boosting the dollar, prompting some in the market to see this as one hope for the dollar now. But few dealers seemed to think such action was a likely option at the moment. \par \par \uc2  "Though there is no doubt that the Fed now appears to be somewhat more receptive to the idea of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  than before, they also don't appear to be so concerned about the (current) dollar level," said Manabu Nakagawa, director of investment banking at the Bank of Nova Scotia. \par \par \uc2  "Timing is everything. If the Fed came in at a strange time and the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed, the market would lose confidence in this as a possible remedy. This is not yet the time," he added, saying he sees the dollar stuck in a range of 87 to 89 yen over the next few weeks. \par \par \uc2  Another recent point of concern has been credit policy. The United States lowered its Federal Funds rate by 25 basis points on July 6, while there had been widespread rumours in the Japanese money market that Japan's official discount rate might be lowered last Friday or on Monday. \par \par \uc2  But dealers discounted rate cuts as a near-term option. "If Japan cuts its discount rate (from the current record low of one percent), they will have no leeway to cut it after that, making this their option of last resort," said Akira Kurihara, chief spot dealer at the Norinchukin Bank. \par \par \uc2  He said the dollar is likely to be range-bound between 86 and 90 yen over the next few weeks, while Bank of America's Hui saw it moving between 87 and 89 yen. \par \par \uc2  Although the markets will watch this Friday's release of U.S. second-quarter gross domestic product (GDP) data for clues to U.S. credit policy, few dealers see the figures having much of an impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  "Recent comments from Fed officials such as (Chairman Alan) Greenspan suggest that even though there is a chance of an easing, it won't be anytime soon. The GDP numbers will probably be as the market expects, with no real signs of inflation, and thus have little impact," Nova Scotia's Nakagawa said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr7p05jm5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
