\par\par \b \uc2  World  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  -3: Dlr Seen Stuck in Ranges For Now \b0\par\par\uc2 226 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juli 1995\par 23:55\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  While some analysts think expectations for a German rate cut may be a wishful fantasy, 'right now the market is looking for anything' that can move it, said Weinberg of High Frequency Economics. \par \par \uc2  As the dollar sank against the yen, traders turned increasingly gloomy about the dollar's long-term prospects. The dollar moved in tight ranges, and traders said those ranges hadn't shifted for months. Accordingly, they said, many investors stayed on the sidelines. \par \par \uc2  'People have just given up,' said Louis Quilici, a vice president at First Fidelity Bank Corp. in Newark, N.J. \par \par \uc2  'Right now the frustration is that we're locked in ranges and there's nothing - except something totally unexpected, like a bomb dropped in Frankfurt - to take us out,' he said. \par \par \uc2  Traders also fretted that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts to prop up the dollar are likely to peter out. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to keep up its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  much longer, many traders said. And the U.S. Federal Reserve has been considerably less aggressive in propping up the dollar. \par \par \uc2  'The bottom line is (U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman) Alan Greenspan is concerned only with John Q. Public and keeping his job even if the dollar is down,' Quilici said. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 24-07-95 \par \par \uc2  2155GMT \par \par \uc2  1033 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025dr7o05l1q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
